/**

\page tarch_logging Logging, tracing, profiling and assertions

\tableofcontents

\section tarch_logging_architecture Logging, Tracing And Assertion Architecture

Peano is built upon a technical architecture (`tarch`) which provides
logging, tracing and assertions. The relevant files are
`tarch/instrumentation/Log.h`, the level specific macro headers
`Debug.h`, `Info.h`, `Warning.h`, `Error.h` and `Trace.h`,
`tarch/instrumentation/CommandLineSink.h`,
`tarch/instrumentation/FileSink.h`, `tarch/instrumentation/Watch.h`,
`tarch/instrumentation/Measurement.h` and `Assertions.h`.

When we build Peano, diagnostics are controlled by three independent
compile-time switches:

-   If a code is translated with `-DPEANO_TRACE=0`,
    `-DPEANO_ASSERTIONS=0` and `-DPEANO_DEBUG=0`,
    debug logging, tracing and assertion macros are disabled, i.e. replaced by
    empty statements. Warnings, information and error messages are still
    displayed.

-   If a code is translated with `-DPEANO_TRACE=1`, `traceEnter()` and
    `traceLeave()` emit timed events into a trace file. Both macros can take
    an explicit label string for function local trace scopes. Peano's `Trace`
    build mode enables `PEANO_TRACE`, keeps `PEANO_ASSERTIONS=0` and
    `PEANO_DEBUG=0`, and defines `NDEBUG`.

-   If a code is translated with `-DPEANO_ASSERTIONS=1`, Peano's assertions are
    enabled, i.e. assertion macros do not degenerate to nop but actually run
    tests. This might increase the runtime significantly as tests run
    permanently. Assertions do not imply trace file output.

-   If a code is translated with `-DPEANO_DEBUG=1`, debug-only data,
    consistency checks and debug messages are compiled in. Debug messages are
    still filtered at runtime before the message is constructed. This avoids
    unbounded file and terminal output in full simulation runs.

\section tarch_logging_interface Using The Logging And Tracing Interface

Peano relies on a plain interface to write out user information. All
constituents of this interface are collected in the package
`tarch::instrumentation`. The usage paradigm is simple:

1.  Logging macros can be used directly. They derive the scope label from the
    compiler function signature at the call site, including namespace and class
    information when the function is implemented in a namespace or class.
2.  The macro argument is a stream expression.

Peano offers user facing logging macros and separate trace instrumentation:

-   **Info**. Should be used to inform your user about the application's
    state.

-   **Warning**. Should be used to inform your user about suspicious
    behaviour. The MPI code uses it, e.g., if many messages arrive in a
    different order than expected. Messages written to the warning level
    are piped to `cerr` and highlighted in yellow on interactive terminals.

-   **Error**. Should be used for errors. It is piped to `cerr` and
    highlighted in bold red on interactive terminals. `logError(...)` does not
    terminate the process.

-   **Tracing**. `traceEnter()` and `traceLeave()` mark timed instrumentation
    scopes. The macros also accept a label string, e.g.
    `traceEnter("Source term evaluation")`. Trace events are not forwarded to
    the command line or file sinks. They are accumulated by rank, thread and
    call hierarchy, and dumped to `Trace.json` and `Trace.html` at shutdown.

-   **Debug**. Should be used to write debug data. All debug data is removed
    unless you translate with `PEANO_DEBUG`. Debug messages are runtime
    filtered before the message is constructed. If enabled, they are written to
    the file dump and stdout.

\section tarch_logging_backends Logging Devices, Formats, Backends And Profiling Support

The logging macros forward information to tarch::instrumentation::Log. Log
formats each line once and then dispatches it to the configured sinks. The
tarch::instrumentation::CommandLineSink remains the default terminal sink.
The same formatted line is also forwarded to tarch::instrumentation::FileSink,
which writes `peano.log` in the process working directory by default. Info,
Warning, Error and Debug messages are forwarded to the file sink. Trace events
are not handled by either the command line sink or the file sink. The file sink
flushes every write.

Warning and Error messages are highlighted only in the terminal sink and only
if the target stream is an interactive terminal. Redirected terminal output and
file output stay plain text without ANSI escape sequences.
Stdout-style messages, such as Info and Debug, are written only by the global
master rank. The same rank filter applies to the file sink. Stderr-style
messages, such as Warning and Error, remain visible from every rank.

Trace instrumentation uses `traceEnter()` and `traceLeave()` pairs and is
compiled only if `PEANO_TRACE=1`. The macros derive the event name from the
compiler function signature at the call site and accumulate inclusive and
exclusive timings per rank, thread and call path. The trace dump is written to
`Trace.json` at shutdown; the same data is rendered as a static `Trace.html`
table with bars and millisecond values. Each traced call path appears once per
rank and thread with its call count, accumulated time, average time per call,
and percentage of the thread's traced runtime.

Function local trace scopes can be nested below the current function by passing
the same label to `traceEnter(...)` and `traceLeave(...)`:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
traceEnter();
traceEnter("Source term evaluation");
evaluateSourceTerm();
traceLeave("Source term evaluation");
traceLeave();
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

In the trace output this produces a child entry named `Source term evaluation`
below the function scope.

Debug logging has an additional runtime filter. The simplest way to configure
it for a simulation run is the `PEANO_LOG_DEBUG` environment variable:

-   Unset, empty, `0`, `false`, `off` or `no`: disable debug messages.

-   `1`, `true`, `on`, `yes`, `all` or `*`: enable all debug messages.

-   Otherwise: comma separated list of scope prefixes. A debug message is
    emitted if its scope name starts with one of these prefixes, e.g.
    `PEANO_LOG_DEBUG=exahype2::TimeStepping,tarch::mpi`.

The same policy can be configured from C++ through
`tarch::instrumentation::Log::setDebug(bool)`,
`tarch::instrumentation::Log::enableDebugScopePrefix(...)` and
`tarch::instrumentation::Log::clearDebugFilter()`.

\subsection tarch_logging_command_line_sink CommandLineSink

This logger is the default terminal sink. It writes already formatted strings
to stdout or stderr and does not coordinate other backends. The formatting and
fan out are configured through tarch::instrumentation::Log. Please consult the
header for details on the semantics of the arguments:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tarch::instrumentation::Log::setLogFormat(
 " ",
 true, false, false, true, true, true,
 "myFancy.log"
);
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The Log interface also allows you to override or disable the file sink. An
empty file name disables file logging. The default is one shared file
independent of the number of MPI ranks, written to the current working
directory from which the executable is launched.

\section tarch_logging_assertions Assertions

Peano provides its own assertion format which is independent of C++
assertions. Assertions are offered through macros, and if you compile with
`PEANO_ASSERTIONS=0`, then they are removed at compile time from your code.

Peano's assertions interlink with the logging, i.e. before they shut
down a rank, they ensure that all logs are flushed. Furthermore, they
provide a few convenient variations that

1.  Support numerical "equality" checks,

2.  Support the logging of parameters, and

3.  Support vector valued comparisons.

It is best to study the macros in tarch/Assertions.h to get an overview.

*/
